---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Listening to Voyager | Claire Nyquist"
    description="A single 18-second radio observation from the Green Bank Telescope, cut into 96 staves and played as a score. Somewhere in the noise is Voyager 1, the only technosignature we have ever confirmed, because we built it."
    image="/images/gifs/seti.webp"
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <!-- Hero -->
        <div class="text-center mb-8 md:mb-12">
            <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-6xl name-gradient mb-4 md:mb-6">
                Listening to Voyager
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
                On September 19th, 2016, the Green Bank Telescope pointed at
                Voyager 1 and recorded five minutes of radio. Below is one
                18-second slice: 28,800 frequency channels, cut into 96 rows
                and stacked. Almost all of it is noise. Three of these lines
                are a spacecraft twenty billion kilometers away, still talking.
                Press play and every line sounds at once, each pitched to its
                own frequency. When the carrier arrives you will know.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- ===== The plate ===== -->
        <section class="my-8">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50">
                    <button
                        id="seti-play"
                        disabled
                        class="px-4 py-1.5 rounded-md bg-gray-900 text-white text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-700 disabled:opacity-40 disabled:cursor-default transition"
                        >Play ▶</button
                    >
                    <button
                        id="seti-restart"
                        class="px-3 py-1.5 rounded-md bg-white border border-gray-300 text-gray-700 text-sm hover:bg-gray-100 transition"
                        >Restart</button
                    >
                    <span class="mx-1 text-gray-300">|</span>
                    <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">Speed:</span>
                    <button
                        data-speed="1"
                        data-active="true"
                        class="seti-sp px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md bg-white border border-gray-300 text-gray-700 text-sm hover:bg-gray-100 data-[active=true]:bg-gray-900 data-[active=true]:text-white data-[active=true]:border-gray-900 transition"
                        >1×</button
                    >
                    <button
                        data-speed="2"
                        class="seti-sp px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md bg-white border border-gray-300 text-gray-700 text-sm hover:bg-gray-100 data-[active=true]:bg-gray-900 data-[active=true]:text-white data-[active=true]:border-gray-900 transition"
                        >2×</button
                    >
                    <button
                        data-speed="4"
                        class="seti-sp px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md bg-white border border-gray-300 text-gray-700 text-sm hover:bg-gray-100 data-[active=true]:bg-gray-900 data-[active=true]:text-white data-[active=true]:border-gray-900 transition"
                        >4×</button
                    >
                    <span class="ml-auto text-xs text-gray-400">🔊 sound on</span>
                </div>
                <div class="px-2 py-3 sm:px-4">
                    <p id="seti-loading" class="text-sm text-gray-400 py-16 text-center">
                        Loading 28,800 channels…
                    </p>
                    <div id="seti-canvas" class="w-full"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">
                Hover any row for its exact frequency, power and channel number.
                Click anywhere on the plate to start the sweep from that column.
                Built with p5.js and the Web Audio API from Breakthrough Listen
                open data.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- About this data -->
        <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                About this data
            </h3>
            <div class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-2">
                <p>
                    A radio telescope measures how much power arrives at each
                    frequency. This plate covers 80.5 kilohertz of sky in
                    channels 2.79 hertz wide, read left to right, top row
                    highest in frequency. Height is power in decibels above the
                    recording&rsquo;s median. There is no binning, smoothing or
                    interpolation: every vertex is one raw channel, which is why
                    the texture never repeats. Noise never repeats.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The bright line in row 48 is Voyager&rsquo;s carrier, 14.6 dB
                    up and only five channels wide. Nothing in nature is that
                    narrow, and narrowness is the whole signature of a
                    transmitter. Rows 21 and 75 are its telemetry sidebands,
                    sitting symmetrically about 22.5 kilohertz either side.
                    Noise does not arrange itself in matched pairs around a
                    center.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The sound is the same numbers, mapped once. Each row is a
                    voice pitched to its own radio frequency; the height of the
                    line sets its loudness and bends its pitch upward. The piece
                    writes itself, and the only reason it has a climax is that a
                    spacecraft is out there.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Observation <span class="font-mono text-xs">Voyager1.single_coarse.fine_res.fil</span>,
                    Green Bank Telescope X-band, 2016-09-19, retrieved September
                    9th, 2026 from the
                    <a href="https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/opendatasearch" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Breakthrough Listen Open Data Archive</a>,
                    which releases its data into the public domain. Recorder and
                    the Voyager commissioning detections described in
                    <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/1538-3873/aa80d2" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MacMahon et al. (2017)</a>.
                    Built with p5.js and the Web Audio API, with Claude Opus 5
                    as a programming assistant.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- p5 + sketch (loaded inline, in document order) -->
        <script is:inline src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@1.9.4/lib/p5.min.js"></script>
        <script is:inline src="/seti-ridgeline-sketch.js"></script>
    </div>
</BaseLayout>
